  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Show that $\\log_{a^x}(b^x)=\\log_a(b)$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\log_a(b)$\n\nChange base and use the power law to cancel the common factor $x$.",
      "worked": "For the given logarithms to be defined, $a>0$, $a\\ne1$, $b>0$ and $x\\ne0$.\n$$\\begin{align*}\\log_{a^x}(b^x)&=\\frac{\\log_a(b^x)}{\\log_a(a^x)}\\\\\\log_{a^x}(b^x)&=\\frac{x\\log_a(b)}{x}\\\\\\log_{a^x}(b^x)&=\\log_a(b).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 23
  },
